# Everyday use

Once a tracker is [set up](/docs/trackers/setting-up), day-to-day use comes down
to two things: start it when you put something new on, and complete it when you
take it off. If your pump or loop already reports those changes, Nocturne can
do both for you.

Running trackers are listed on the **Active** tab under **Settings >
Notifications & Trackers**. Each row shows how long it has left, how old it is
and when it started.

<Screenshot id="trackers-active" />

## Starting a tracker

Use **Start Tracker** on the Active tab, **Start** on a row of the Definitions
tab, or one of your [presets](/docs/trackers/setting-up#presets). The Start box
asks for:

- **Start Time**: defaults to now. Set it earlier if you changed your sensor
  this morning and are only logging it now. The tracker counts from this time,
  not from when you pressed the button.
- **Notes**: optional. A site, a lot number, anything you might want to look
  back on.

A duration tracker with notification steps also shows a preview of when each
step will fire, worked out from the start time you chose.

An event tracker asks for **Scheduled For** instead: the date and time of the
appointment or refill it counts down to.

Starting a duration tracker ends the one already running, so you never have
two copies each sending their own reminders. The old one is completed at the
new start time, as **Replaced Early**, or as **Expired** if it had reached its
lifespan. If you want to record a different reason, complete it yourself
first.

Nocturne refuses a start dated earlier than the one already running, because
it would end a newer sensor to log an older one. Complete the newer one first
if that is really what you mean. Event trackers are the exception: you can
have several booked at once, such as two appointments.

## Completing a tracker

Press **Complete** on the tracker's row, or **Complete Tracker** in the panel
that opens when you tap its pill on the home screen.

<Screenshot id="tracker-complete-dialog" />

- **Completed At**: when the change actually happened.
- **Completion Reason**: why it ended. The choices depend on the category.

  | Category | Reasons offered |
  |----------|-----------------|
  | Sensor, Cannula, Consumable | Fell Off, Replaced Early, plus the general ones |
  | Reservoir | Empty, Refilled, plus the general ones |
  | Appointment | Attended, Rescheduled, Cancelled, Missed, plus the general ones |
  | Battery, Reminder, Custom | The general ones: Completed, Failed, Expired, Other |

- **Notes**: optional. For example, "sensor error on day 8".
- **Start another**: starts a fresh run of the same tracker at the completion
  time. Tick it when you are replacing like for like.

If the tracker is set to
[write an event on completion](/docs/trackers/setting-up#writing-events-for-nightscout-apps),
this is when that event is created. If the same tracker also
[restarts automatically](#automatic-restarts) on that event, a new run starts
from it, just as if your pump had reported the change.

Picking an honest reason is what makes the history useful. "Failed" and "Fell
Off" are worth recording even when they are annoying. Over a few months they
show whether a particular sensor batch or wear site is giving you trouble,
which is useful to take to your care team.

## Automatic restarts

Most pumps and loops already report when you change something. Loop, AAPS, Trio
and many connectors send events such as `Site Change` or `Sensor Start`, and so
does anything that uploads Nightscout treatments. A tracker can listen for
those events and restart on its own.

When a matching event arrives, Nocturne:

1. Completes the running tracker at the time of the event. The reason is
   **Expired** if it reached its lifespan, **Replaced Early** if it did not, and
   **Completed** if it has no lifespan. The note says which event did it.
2. Starts a new run from the same moment.

Nocturne ignores the same event arriving twice. It also ignores an older event
arriving late, so a connector filling in history cannot wind your tracker back
to a sensor you have already taken off. Only duration trackers restart this
way. Event trackers are tied to a date, so a device event cannot restart them.

Choose the events in the tracker editor, under
[Restart automatically on](/docs/trackers/setting-up#restarting-automatically).
The choices are `Sensor Start`, `Sensor Change`, `Sensor Stop`,
`Transmitter Sensor Insert`, `Site Change`, `Cannula Change`, `Pod Change`,
`Insulin Change`, `Reservoir Change` and `Pump Battery Change`.

If two trackers listen for the same event, such as two infusion sites that are
both logged as `Site Change`, give each one a notes filter. The tracker then
only restarts when the event's notes contain that text, for example "left" or
"right".

## On the home screen

Running trackers appear as a row of pills beside your current reading. Each
one shows the tracker's name and how long it has been running. The thin line
under it shows how much of its lifespan is used up. The age turns the colour of
the most urgent step reached, and gets a warning mark from Warning upwards. A
step lights the pill at the same moment its alert fires, so a "24 hours before
the end" step changes the colour a day before the end, not before.
[Dashboard visibility](/docs/trackers/setting-up#on-the-dashboard) decides
which trackers appear here, and when.

<Screenshot id="tracker-pill-bar" />

Tap a pill for the details: running time, expected lifespan, time remaining,
your start notes and when it started. **Complete Tracker** at the bottom opens
the same Complete box as the trackers page.

<Screenshot id="tracker-pill-popover" />

Trackers also appear on the **Calendar**, and the glucose chart can mark when a
tracker is due. Turn that on or off from the chart legend.

## When a reminder fires

A tracker reminder arrives like your other alerts, on the channels its alert
rule uses (see
[where the notification goes](/docs/trackers/setting-up#where-the-notification-goes)).
Acknowledging it marks it as seen, the same as any other alert. That covers the
steps the tracker has already reached. The next step on the ladder will still
notify you when its time comes.

To make every reminder for a tracker stop, complete the tracker. A tracker that
is not running has nothing to remind you about.

## History

The **History** tab lists finished trackers, newest first. Each entry shows how
long it ran, why it ended, any completion notes and when it ended. Trackers
that restarted automatically are listed too, with the event that restarted
them.
